faqs:
  - q: "Do I legally need interconnected alarms?"
    a: "If you're building new, yes — since late 2023 the Building Code's F7 acceptable solution calls for interconnected smoke alarms in new homes, and many consented renovations trigger the same rule. For an existing home there's no requirement to upgrade, but once you've heard a whole-house system in action it's hard to go back. For sleeping kids behind closed doors, it's a genuinely different level of protection."
  - q: "Wireless or hardwired — which should I get?"
    a: "For existing homes, wireless interconnection wins almost every time: no cutting into ceilings, no cabling, and the alarms pair with each other over radio. Install takes about an hour and the house stays tidy. Hardwired interconnection makes sense for new builds and big renovations where the walls are already open. Both do the same thing when it matters — every alarm sounds together."
  - q: "How many interconnected alarms does my house need?"
    a: "Same placement logic as any install — every bedroom, hallways, living areas, every level — the difference is they all talk to each other. A typical two-storey 4-bedroom home lands around 6 to 7 alarms. We quote the exact number after a quick chat about your layout, and the price always includes the alarms themselves."
  - q: "Will they false alarm together? That sounds like chaos."
    a: "Fair concern — one burnt sausage setting off seven alarms would get old fast. Two things prevent it: we fit photoelectric units, which mostly ignore cooking, and we keep alarms out of false-alarm zones near kitchens and bathrooms. The hush button on any alarm also silences the lot. In practice, false alarms across our interconnected installs are genuinely rare."
